IMPORTANT NOTES
---------------
1. Gene ID format: OrthoFinder stores Astrangia genes as
   "protein|evm.model.Ap11.XXXX" — scripts strip this prefix
   automatically to match the "Ap11.XXXX" format in DTW files.

2. The broad run detects MORE orthogroups than cnidaria-only
   because bilaterians act as "bridges" connecting coral genes
   through shared sequence similarity. Use broad run as primary.

3. Stability = same Astrangia genes cluster together in both runs.
   Split = adding taxa revealed hidden paralogy (biologically valid).

4. Orphan genes (no orthogroup in either run) are candidates for
   Astrangia-specific innovations. Consider BLASTing against NCBI nr
   to confirm they are truly novel.

TO RUN ON A NEW DATASET
-----------------------
1. Replace DTW_clusters_with_annotations.tsv with your gene+cluster file
   (must have columns: gene_id, cluster)
2. Update proteome paths in each script
3. Change the species column name grep pattern if not "apoculata/poculata"
4. Change the gene ID prefix stripping regex if not "protein|evm.model."
5. Run scripts in order: stability → rescue → conservation_matrix

